    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `check_server(server, cases)` in `mcpcheck/inproc.py`. All 3 tests pass with `pytest -q`: the 2 that were already there and 1 new one.\n\n**How it connects:** I checked the docs and the installed mcp 2.2.0 package. It passes the server to the SDK's own `Client` with `mode=\"legacy\"`. That starts the server as a background task and connects a `ClientSession` to it over in-memory streams, with no subprocess and no sockets. It accepts a decorator-built `MCPServer` as well as a low-level `Server`.\n\n**Why it matches `check_command`:**\n- **Same handshake and messages:** `mode=\"legacy\"` sends the same `initialize` handshake and JSON-RPC messages as the stdio path. The default mode would skip both on an in-process server, so results could differ from stdio.\n- **Same tool call:** each case goes through `client.session.call_tool(...)`, the same `ClientSession.call_tool` that `check_command` uses. The higher-level `Client.call_tool` would add extra steps, such as automatically answering servers that ask for more input.\n- **No caching:** I turned off the client's response cache (`cache=None`), so every case actually reaches the server.\n- **Same comparison and order:** it runs the cases in order and uses the same `compare()`, so the mismatches come back in the same order.\n\nNo dependencies were added.\n\n**Test:** `tests/test_inproc.py` builds a small server with the `@server.tool()` decorator and runs four cases, two passing and two failing. It checks that exactly the two failures come back, in order, with the text the server actually returned."
  },
